= Introduction <sec:intro>

== Motivation <sec:intro-motivation>

*Warehouse fleets jam.* Automated warehouses move goods with fleets of mobile robots that share narrow aisles, intersections and pick stations. Even in the planning literature, which treats the warehouse as a stream of path finding problems, congestion is a first-order concern: routing each robot along its free-flow shortest path creates congestion @chen2023traffic, and a solver that plans over a bounded horizon can leave two agents in a corridor waiting for each other @li2020lifelong. When robots meet head-on in an aisle, or several reach a junction together, the fleet can stop altogether. Such a deadlock does not dissolve on its own: each robot holds the floor it stands on while it waits for floor held by another, the structure of the classical "deadly embrace" @coffman1971system.

*Local reactive control cannot break the symmetry.* Many robots navigate with a local stack such as Nav2 @macenski2020marathon, which plans for one robot at a time and treats other robots as obstacles. Reactive methods keep each robot safe, but they decide how to avoid a collision, not who should give way. A planner can conclude that every path forward is unsafe and stop although a feasible path exists @trautman2010unfreezing, and for controllers built on control barrier functions deadlock is an equilibrium on the boundary of the safe set @grover2019deadlock. Two robots that face each other and run the same recovery policy can recreate the stall they are trying to escape (@sec:bg-reactive).

*A slow semantic layer could supply what is missing.* The proposed remedies for reactive deadlock share one structure: a slower layer sets an order or a timing, and a faster layer keeps the motion safe (@sec:bg-reactive). The slow layer needs a view of the whole situation, which a fixed overhead camera provides. Vision-language models (VLMs), which read an image with a text description and answer in a structured form, are candidates for turning that view into a decision: they can name the kind of jam and choose a way out from a closed vocabulary of strategies. Closely related work already calls a VLM on a stall, shows it a top-view image and uses it as a high-level planner above a safe low-level controller @garg2024foundation @garg2026online.

*A model's plan cannot be trusted as it stands.* Language models do not plan reliably on their own @valmeekam2023planning, and the emerging position is to treat their output as a candidate that external, model-based checks must verify @kambhampati2024llms. A VLM is also slow, costly per call and fallible: it may time out, refuse, return malformed output, or name a yielder with no room to back up. The design question is therefore not only whether a VLM can recognise a jam, but how its decision becomes commands that are safe to execute, and how much of the executed plan can still be credited to the model.

== Problem Statement <sec:intro-problem>

This report studies a hybrid, two-layer coordinator for a warehouse robot fleet. *Local navigation* (Layer 1) is fast and per robot: each robot runs its own Nav2 stack, with a design target of 20–50 Hz #ev("1.01"). The *coordination layer* (Layer 2) is the slow, centralised loop, with a design target of 0.5–2 Hz #ev("1.01"), that detects a stall, asks the VLM, grounds the decision and dispatches commands; with the *coordination layer off*, only each robot's local navigation runs. When the stall detector reports that robots have stopped making progress, the orchestrator sends the annotated overhead frame and the tracked robot states to the VLM, which classifies the congestion and chooses one of eight strategies #ev("1.02"). The *grounder*, a deterministic, geometry-driven layer, turns the decision into robot commands: it checks feasibility, repairs or replaces an infeasible plan, and refuses when no safe manoeuvre exists. Only the yielder's retreat room is checked against static obstacles; escort segments and reverse legs are not (@sec:design-grounding). An independent safety monitor enforces robot-to-robot distance and speed limits on every command, with one exemption for clearing manoeuvres (@sec:design-safety).

One property of this design shapes every claim in the report: the command the robots execute is always the grounder's output. The one exception, an opt-in switch that lets a verified model plan set the order of the back-out phases, was used only in the demonstration campaign. Each dispatch carries a *plan-audit label*: the model's plan was *accepted unchanged*, *repaired*, or replaced by the *grounder default*. The label records how the model's proposal fared in the audit, not which commands came from it; in the earlier live pilot on the intersection, every plan the model proposed was repaired or replaced #ev("1.13"). Claims are therefore made about the pipeline, and the model's share is measured separately.

The problem addressed is the following. _Can a pipeline in which a stall trigger detects congestion, a VLM given the annotated overhead frame and a text list of robot states confirms and classifies it, and a deterministic grounder turns its decision into safe commands, clear congestion that local reactive control leaves in place, and what does each part contribute?_ It is posed for a small simulated fleet and answered with one registered pilot, exploratory runs and post hoc decision-level analyses. Any success is credited to the pipeline (VLM and grounding together), not to the model alone. The project proposal framed a broader comparison against classical geometric coordinators; the final experiments instead compare the pipeline with the coordination layer off on the same code, and with a scripted arm that skips the VLM call and runs the grounder's default plan (@sec:intro-scope).

== Research Questions <sec:intro-rq>

The research design frozen with the first registration states five research questions #ev("1.03"), defined in @sec:ch5-rq:

- *RQ1, perception and classification.* Does the VLM classify the congestion type and the kind of blocker correctly, and tell congestion apart from normal flow? Because the VLM is called only after the stall trigger fires, RQ1 concerns post-trigger classification and false positives, not open-world detection.
- *RQ2, strategy competence.* Do the strategies the VLM chooses clear congestion?
- *RQ3, social reasoning.* Are the robot the VLM names to yield, and the crossing order it proposes, effective?
- *RQ4, semantic advantage.* Does the VLM use information that no classical baseline has, such as what a blocker is?
- *RQ5, closed-loop adaptation.* Does feedback about a failed command change the VLM's next decision? (Here "closed loop" means re-analysis that receives feedback about the failed command, not the pipeline cycle defined in @sec:intro-contributions.)

The studies that were run answer narrower questions, which Chapter 5 states per study (@sec:ch5-arms). @tab:intro-rq-map maps each study to the questions it informs; the studies are kept apart and never pooled.

