The studies do not test every registered question. RQ4 and RQ5 are not tested. RQ2 and RQ3 are not tested against the comparators registered for them in the first design (a per-type policy table, and a first-come-first-served rule with a commitment tiebreak), because from the third design on the control is the coordination layer switched off. RQ3 is informed only at the decision level. Pilot probes showed that the robot one model named to yield followed the robot listed first in the prompt, which under ascending listing is also the lowest identifier, so the listing order was randomised and recorded from then on #ev("1.12"); the later live comparisons nevertheless listed robots in ascending order #ev("1.15"), so their yielder choices cannot separate the two priors.

== Contributions <sec:intro-contributions>

The system and method contributions are artefacts that exist and have been run:

+ *A working closed loop in simulation.* Here and below, "closed loop" means the full cycle of detect, decide, ground, act and resolve. The coordinator runs end to end in Isaac Sim with ROS 2, Nav2 and a hosted VLM: overhead ArUco tracking produces robot states, a stall detector decides when to ask, the VLM decides, the grounder produces commands, and an execution layer carries them out and reports back (Chapters 3 and 4). One configuration key switches the coordination layer off, which gives the experiments a local-navigation-only control on identical code (@sec:design-modules).
+ *A deterministic grounder with per-dispatch plan-audit labels.* The grounder checks each decision against scene geometry and robot positions, hands a yield to another robot when the named yielder has no room to back up, refuses when no robot has, and audits the model's plan with a bounded repair search (@sec:design-grounding). Verify-and-repair of model plans is not new (@sec:bg-grounding); what the closest published systems do not report, as far as we found, is the per-dispatch audit label as an experimental quantity, together with a re-grounding of each decision without its plan (@sec:bg-comparison).
+ *A safety layer that does not depend on the model.* An independent monitor holds any robot pair closer than 0.45 m and any robot above its speed limit, and treats a robot whose marker is lost as unsafe; the live experiments add a hard-contact floor at 0.20 m centre distance that applies even inside the clearing-manoeuvre exemption #ev("1.04"). A review after the experiments found the exemption's scope wider than intended, and the evaluated runtime does not contain the fix (@sec:design-safety). The safety evidence is robot-pair centre distance only: the simulator's contact fields were a stub #ev("6.20"), and neither the monitor nor the grounder checks escort segments or reverse legs against static obstacles.
+ *Registered designs.* The design was registered three times, the first tagged on 30 September 2026 and the third dated 6 October 2026, with later changes as numbered, append-only addenda #ev("1.05"). Every scored criterion was fixed before its data were collected, and post hoc analyses are labelled as such (@sec:ch5-prereg).
+ *An offline decision benchmark.* It replays 187 recorded analyses from 31 live episodes #ev("1.06") through the production grounder and ranks prompt and input variants by what would happen downstream, without simulating any episode (@sec:ch5-bench).
+ *A physical demonstration.* On 12 November 2026 the detect-to-dispatch chain ran on physical TurtleBot3 robots under an overhead camera, in four staged arrangements of up to four robots, with a different model and no navigation goals #ev("1.07") (@sec:impl-physical). It shows that the chain runs on hardware; it is not an evaluation.

== Summary of Findings <sec:intro-findings>

In intersection run 1 the full pipeline cleared the four-robot jam in 4 of 5 episodes, against 0 of 5 with the coordination layer off; this is an exploratory pilot with five episodes per arm (@sec:res-ib10-ab) #ev("6.05"). In intersection run 2 the scripted default-plan arm cleared it in 4 of 4 valid episodes and the live-VLM arm in 5 of 5, and every first dispatch of the live arm re-grounds to the scripted arm's plan, so the clearing is credited to the trigger and the grounder, not to the model's plan; the run is exploratory and never pooled with run 1 (@sec:res-ib10-c) #ev("6.06"), and a trace of all live four-robot analyses found that the model's plan changed the dispatched commands in 0 of 39 that pass its fidelity gate (@sec:res-plan) #ev("6.02"). In the registered five-robot pilot no episode in either arm completed all 25 tasks (0 of 10), and the throughput difference, on minus off, was +0.032 with a bootstrap interval of [−0.008, +0.080] that includes zero, a pilot estimate from five seed pairs (@sec:res-t1) #ev("6.14"). In the two-robot head-on scenes the grounded yielder was made correct by the grounder's feasibility gate, which overturned 37 of 79 model choices, not by the model, and in pre-registered offline replays no tested model met the rule for choosing the yielder from the scene; both are decision-level analyses, the first post hoc (@sec:res-headon) #ev("6.08") #ev("6.09"). In the five-robot scene, re-grounding the recorded analyses showed that 78 analyses gave 13 motion commands and 47 were withheld by grounding refusals #ev("6.15"), but the exploratory failure-cause classification names no dominant cause, and 27 of 55 failure units stay undetermined (@sec:res-failure-causes) #ev("6.63"). The demonstration campaign is illustrative and yields no rates: the model returned a valid decision in all 67 analyses, its plan was recorded as accepted unchanged in 6 of 65 plan-channel analyses (5 after an offline check of the command actually sent), in the intersection, the only scene where this was computed, it changed the executed commands once, by reordering two near-equivalent back-outs, and an escort path with no obstacle check drove one robot into a rack island (@sec:res-demo) #ev("6.44") #ev("6.51") #ev("6.01").

== Scope and Non-Goals <sec:intro-scope>

The system uses one fixed overhead camera and planar poses; multi-camera fusion and six-degree-of-freedom pose are out of scope. The coordinator clears one detected conflict at a time with bounded manoeuvres and hands control back to the robots' planners; it does not replan the fleet as a multi-agent path finding solver would @stern2019multi, and it is not in the fast control loop. No classical coordinator (potential fields, reciprocal collision avoidance, conflict-based search) serves as control, so the experiments measure pipeline on against pipeline off, not a VLM against a classical method (@sec:ch5-threats); energy use is not measured. Every scored episode ran in Isaac Sim, with one model in the live comparisons and one pinned runtime. Samples are small; each study's evidence grade, size and pooling rule are listed once, in @tab:ch5-studies. Extensions built after the pinned runtime are not evaluated (@sec:impl-later), and no result is generalised beyond its scene, model and runtime.

== Report Outline <sec:intro-outline>

Chapter 2 reviews related work and states the research gap. Chapter 3 presents the system design, and Chapter 4 its implementation. Chapter 5 sets out the studies and how they were run and analysed. Chapter 6 reports the results, Chapter 7 discusses what they support and their limits, and Chapter 8 concludes with future work. The Evidence Index appendix maps every number in the text to its source.
